[Music]  early morning in mainland Europe two  white vans are quietly being loaded full  of cocaine Ashish an ecstasy they're  being ready to move nearly four tons of  narcotics authorities have been trying  for decades to contain the global trade  in illicit drugs the drugs in these vans  will be taken to an incinerator and  destroyed  it's a journey the Portuguese police  make every month and they know there's  no end in sight our job is to try to  make it as hard as possible for the drug  traffickers to sell their product but  it's not a fight that we can win this is  the story of how one country sparked a  global movement for change  [Music]  at a Portuguese incineration plant the  drug squad prepares to burn their latest  haul for years Portugal took a hard line  approach to drugs  the policy was that all people caught  with drugs regardless of the amount and  even if they wore for their own personal  use will be legally prosecuted as drug  traffickers they will be brought into  court and they may be sentenced to some  time in prison but the hardline approach  didn't make a difference regarding  people stopping use drugs in the 1990s  drug use spread to every part of society  in this small European country one in  every hundred of the population were  believed to be addicted to heroin  simply incinerating their supply was no  answer the country needed to forge a  radical new approach to drugs in 2001  Portugal came up with a policy that  would put its people first a new law  enables citizens to possess small  amounts of any illegal drug it would no  longer be a criminal offense for mostly  casual users to enjoy their vice of  choice state resources could be focused  on addict's instead of being punished  for their dependency they'd be offered  help that it was old Factory and they  are using their psychologists Maria and  Raquel a state-funded outreach workers  everyday they visit drug users to offer  clean needles smoking pipes and advice  they call this approach harm reduction  we respect their space you know we are  not like come on get to treatment so you  have to stop it's not what we say you  know in their time if they want we we  help them to go to treatment  decriminalization has removed much of  the stigma addicts felt and the fear of  prosecution that stopped them from  seeking help it's easy to them to get to  us and say ok I have a drug problem and  I need help they don't have to lie to us  it's it's like ok I'm using and I'm not  afraid to get get in jail or something  like that and I think this is a big big  difference you know  [Music]  90% of Portugal's anti-drugs resources  are now spent on treatment and  prevention 10% on policing and  punishment in the United States it's the  opposite side to my talks of Indian  cinema twinsen intimidated  but the Hoopoe was wanted in 5% opinion  he did know I stood up for the Cape's  wanna say shake up sua since  decriminalization drug-induced deaths  have dropped from 80 in 2001 to just 16  in 2012 over the same period the total  number of heroin addicts and haft  Portugal's experiment defied the fears  of opponents and broke a global policy  taboo but for all its success the new  approach did nothing to address the  supply side of the drugs trade though  citizens weren't now being arrested for  possessing drugs  the police were still chasing their  dealers globally criminal gangs and  drugs cartels continued to dominate a  drugs trade with over 300 billion  dollars  [Music]  the countries where those drugs  originated continued to pay the price  they've been paying for over a quarter  of a century in the Colombian capital  Bogota the polls opened with a minute's  silence for the three candidates killed  during the presidential campaign it was  the culmination of weeks of terror  the man expected to win is the Liberal  Party's Cesar Gaviria he arrived  surrounded by bodyguards and a media  posse he's pledged to continue the  present administration's hardline  against the drug cartels the cartels in  Colombia by 1990 became almost  all-powerful Escobar was able to do  terrorism in a way that only probably  bin Laden has been able blowing up  buildings and bringing down planes and  killing policemen one by one he was  really a threat to Colombian democracy  they were scaring times  when he won the election Gaviria vowed  to take on the cartels in his own way  you have to fight CalSTRS you have to  militarize the whole thing to show that  these people could go to jail at that  moment we had to do it  cavalry oversaw the dismantling of  Colombia's drugs cartels and the killing  of their most infamous leader Pablo  Escobar  [Music]  but after four years in office he seemed  to have made little lasting impact  whereas once they grew coca plants for  cocaine production now it's poppy seeds  one kilo of heroin sells in New York for  $160,000 ten times the price of cocaine  the war on drugs really fail and at the  end what you see is no matter what you  do the drugs gets to the US the life of  Colombia has been really really  difficult we have suffered so much  violence you have to look for a way out  you have to look for a solution we know  that just repression just prohibitionism  doesn't work we know it for certain we  had lived that the drug producing  countries of Latin America are following  the lead of Portugal and demanding  change  but it's the country that began the war  on drugs the might just bring it to an  end here in America they're now selling  marijuana in the mall this one's gonna  be a little more earthy definitely a  really good strength in 2014  Colorado became the first US state to  fully legalize cannabis within a year it  had already become a 700 million dollar  business there among the new industries  biggest players medicine man is  testament to the growing impacts of a  new breed of cannabis capitalists when  my brother and I started this and my  cousin Frank was our first employee and  we stood right outside the doors outside  here and we said what do we want to be  and by the end of that discussion he  said we want to be that Costco of  marijuana and at the time we were really  thinking of only cultivation now we're  the leader in this industry in Colorado  and our vision has changed so now we  want to be the Costco of cultivation and  the Starbucks of retail in the last year  we did about nine million this year  we'll do about eighteen million dollars  in business Anandi's purpose-built plant  50 different marijuana strains are grown  trimmed dried and cured to develop their  flavor and potency its cultivation on an  industrial scale yet Colorado law  requires every plant to be tracked  throughout the process from seed to sale  [Music]  legal controls are helping move  marijuana away from the black market and  into mainstream society just as you  would see somebody having a casual drink  on a patio you might see somebody around  the corner just taking a quick hit of  Bowl  [Music]  welcome to the joint ruling class once  you feel like you've got enough there  slowly start rolling it between and move  your fingers in slowly in one room they  had they taught you how to roll a joint  which is really cool I rolled my first  joint today it was pretty awesome and  then after that they taught us how to  roll our own sushi which was really cool  it's kind of great a great mix of people  everyone was really friendly hello hi  [Music]  testament to just how mainstream  marijuana has become is the  fastest-growing part of the industry  known as edibles there's a plethora of  food and drink products designed to  appeal to a public who may never have  smoked infused with cannabis oil edibles  offer an example of how legalization  enabled regulation in 2014  Colorado introduced strict new rules on  the packaging and potency of these  products for the industry it's become a  necessary part of building trust with  the public December of 2013 a couple of  leaders in Colorado came out and gave  warnings that reefer madness was about  to ensue in Colorado and that people  said whoa boy kids are going to be using  marijuana in great numbers and shortly  after legalization they hadn't retract  what they said  [Music]  initial research a suggested marijuana  use by teenagers actually fell meanwhile  the state reaped revenues of 76 million  dollars from fees and taxes on marijuana  sales that set to rise to nearly a  hundred million dollars in 2016 and with  it  the funding of schools and the police  it's been a marked for another great  benefit about this industry is choking  out the black market whether that's the  cartels or or organized crime we are  taking their customers from them and  we're gonna choke them out to almost  non-existing  Colorado's experience helped to trigger  a momentum for change across the United  States  almost half of American states have now  taken some steps to legalize and  regulate cannabis many many people still  say well we don't know what will happen  if this is free if we decriminalize the  production and the consumption of drugs  they they should look at Portugal and  Colorado today is a very legitimate  issue I have no doubt that the world is  going to move to regulation instead of  prohibition is about abandoning  something that does work fourteen years  ago  Portugal's experiments at a bold new  path for dealing with drugs the shift it  prompted could be about to reach a  critical global mass  [Music]

